Bound the compile-time expansion of nested bounded repetitions in TRE.

tre_expand_ast() rewrites each bounded repetition into catenated copies
of its body.  Individual counts are limited to RE_DUP_MAX, but nested
repetitions multiply, so a short pattern such as

    (?:a{2,101,})(?:a{2,101,}){100}{100}

expands to millions of AST nodes and gigabytes of allocation before
compilation finishes or fails.  These allocations bypass the harness's
R vector-heap cap (TRE allocates through malloc, not the R heap), and
the batch runs disable libFuzzer's own limit with -rss_limit_mb=0, so
the regex targets balloon until the kernel steps in: in batch run
33847124756 one of agrep's two fuzzing jobs was OOM-killed (exit 137)
and the survivor finished at 11.9 GB RSS, silently halving that
target's throughput.

Cap the total number of positions in the expanded AST at 100000 and
fail compilation with REG_ESPACE beyond that.  Legitimate patterns are
orders of magnitude below the cap; even (a{255}){255}, the largest
two-level nesting RE_DUP_MAX permits, needs about 65000.

Note this does NOT address the other TRE finding from that run, the
match-time backtracking blowup in tre_tnfa_run_backtrack() on patterns
with backreferences (timeout artifacts in the grep target).  That has
no upstream fix yet; timeouts do not fail the batch.

Upstream status: submitted as r-devel/r-svn PR #294, not yet on the R
Bugzilla as of 2026-09-04.  Delete this patch once the fix lands in R
trunk (ossfuzz.sh will report it as already-applied in the meantime,
which is harmless).

